@node Main "MintPrint Settings Help"
@toc Main
                       MintPrint Settings Help
                       ------------------------

MintPrint Settings is the setup and test program for MintPRINT, the
IPP/AirPrint printer.device driver. It finds your network printer,
asks it what it can do, and saves the settings MintPRINT needs to
print from ordinary Amiga applications.

This guide covers the program window itself. For the driver's own
design notes and printer-by-printer compatibility results, see the
"docs/" folder shipped with MintPRINT's source, or the project's
GitHub page.

Topics:

    @{" Getting Started "        LINK "GettingStarted"}
    @{" Finding your printer "   LINK "Discover"}
    @{" Query Printer "          LINK "Query"}
    @{" Printer Engine "         LINK "Engines"}
    @{" DPI and the 300* option "LINK "DPI"}
    @{" Media and Tray "         LINK "MediaTrays"}
    @{" Duplex (Sides) "         LINK "Duplex"}
    @{" Scaling and Quality "    LINK "ScalingQuality"}
    @{" Multiple printers (Units) " LINK "Units"}
    @{" Installing the driver "  LINK "DriverInstall"}
    @{" Why two driver builds? "  LINK "TwoDrivers"}
    @{" Test Print "             LINK "TestPrint"}
    @{" Troubleshooting "        LINK "Troubleshooting"}
    @{" Reporting a problem "    LINK "ReportingProblems"}

@endnode

@node GettingStarted "Getting Started"
@toc Main
                            Getting Started
                            ---------------

For a brand new printer, the quickest path is:

    1. Run MintPrint Settings.
    2. Click @{" Discover " LINK "Discover"} to search the LAN, or type your
       printer's IPv4 address straight into "Printer IPv4" (hostnames like
       "printer.local" are not resolved - use the numeric address).
    3. Click @{" Query Printer " LINK "Query"}. This asks the printer what it
       supports and fills in Printer Engine, Media, Print Mode, Scaling,
       Quality, DPI and Sides from real capability data.
    4. Check the settings Query filled in look sensible (Printer Engine
       and Media in particular), then click Save.
    5. If MintPRINT is not yet installed as your driver, Settings will
       have already offered to do that - see
       @{" Installing the driver " LINK "DriverInstall"}. Select MintPRINT
       in AmigaOS Printer Preferences and save there too.
    6. Click Test Print to confirm everything actually reaches paper.

Only Unit0 is what the installed driver reads when it prints - see
@{" Multiple printers (Units) " LINK "Units"} if you have more than one
network printer.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node Discover "Finding your printer"
@toc Main
                          Finding your printer
                          --------------------

Clicking Discover runs two scans of your local network, about five
seconds each:

    - SSDP: one UPnP-style multicast search.
    - mDNS: a DNS query for "_ipp._tcp.local", which is how most current
      IPP/AirPrint printers actually advertise themselves.

Any address that replies to either scan appears in a small selection
list. Pick your printer and choose "Use Selected" - this fills in
Printer IPv4 and immediately runs the same query as the Query
button, so the printer's real name, supported formats and other
capabilities appear right away.

Discover is a best-effort LAN scan. A printer behind a router or VLAN
that blocks multicast traffic will not show up even though it works
fine once its address is entered manually. If nothing appears, just
type the printer's IP address into Printer IPv4 and click
@{" Query Printer " LINK "Query"} as usual.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node Query "Query Printer"
@toc Main
                              Query Printer
                              -------------

Query Printer sends an IPP Get-Printer-Attributes request and uses the
real answer to fill in, or narrow down, most of the window:

    - Printer Engine: which of JPEG, PostScript, PWG Raster, PDF and
      Apple Raster (URF) the printer actually advertises - see
      @{" Printer Engine " LINK "Engines"}.
    - Media and tray/source choices.
    - Print Mode (colour/monochrome).
    - Scaling and Quality choices the printer reports.
    - DPI - see @{" DPI and the 300* option " LINK "DPI"}.
    - Sides (duplex), when the selected engine and printer both support
      it - see @{" Duplex (Sides) " LINK "Duplex"}.
    - The printer's make/model, shown once Save has been used at least
      once for this Unit.
    - A short status word next to the "Ink/Toner:" ink levels - "Ready",
      "Busy", "Stopped", or a specific problem such as "Jam", "Door Open",
      "Toner Empty", "Out of Paper" or "Supply Low", read from the
      printer's own reported state. Blank until Query has been run at
      least once.

A field showing "Not Detected" simply means nothing has been queried
and saved yet for the currently selected Unit; it is not an error.

If the printer's advertised format list contains none of the five
formats MintPRINT can produce, a requester points you at filing a
GitHub issue - see @{" Reporting a problem " LINK "ReportingProblems"}.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node Engines "Printer Engine"
@toc Main
                              Printer Engine
                              --------------

MintPRINT can produce five kinds of document:

    JPEG           image/jpeg
    PostScript     application/postscript
    PWG Raster     image/pwg-raster
    PDF            application/pdf
    Apple Raster   image/urf

Any IPP Everywhere or AirPrint-certified printer (most network
printers from roughly the last decade) is required to accept PWG
Raster, so it is usually the safest and cheapest choice - it costs far
less CPU on real 68k hardware than JPEG or PDF, which both go through
a DCT/quantization step. When Query finds a printer that supports PWG
Raster, Settings prefers it automatically unless you have deliberately
pinned a different engine yourself.

Apple Raster (URF) is for printers that advertise none of MintPRINT's
other three raster/JPEG-based formats but do advertise image/urf -
some AirPrint-only devices are like this. It shares PWG Raster's cheap
row compression, so when a printer doesn't advertise PWG Raster but
does advertise URF, Settings prefers URF over JPEG/PDF for the same
reason it prefers PWG Raster when that's available.

PostScript and PDF are there for older or partially-compliant IPP
printers that front an existing office-printer PostScript/PCL
interpreter and reject raster formats outright - some PostScript-only
printers work but are noticeably slower per page than a native raster
printer.

If Settings finds your printer supports PWG Raster while a slower
engine is currently pinned, it will offer, once per session, to switch
you over.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node DPI "DPI and the 300* option"
@toc Main
                          DPI and the 300* option
                          -----------------------

Query normally fills the DPI cycle gadget from the printer's own
reported resolution capabilities. Some printers under-report this
list - for example, a printer may advertise only 600 DPI over IPP but
still print 300-DPI PWG Raster correctly (and considerably faster).

When PWG Raster is selected and the printer reports resolutions but
does not report 300 DPI, Settings adds an extra entry marked
"300* dpi". The asterisk means "compatibility option: not actually
reported by the printer" - it is a deliberate override, not a printer
capability. It is never auto-selected for a brand new, unsaved
profile, but a previously saved 300 DPI choice is kept across future
queries and restarts.

If your printer's real resolution list does not include a working 300
DPI mode, leave DPI on one of the plain (non-starred) entries.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node MediaTrays "Media and Tray"
@toc Main
                              Media and Tray
                              --------------

Query reads the printer's supported media sizes and tray/source names
and fills the Media dropdown with them. Where the printer maps a
particular tray to a particular medium, MintPRINT uses that mapping
automatically; otherwise "auto" is generally the safest source choice.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node Duplex "Duplex (Sides)"
@toc Main
                              Duplex (Sides)
                              --------------

The Sides selector defaults to One-sided and is only enabled once
Query confirms all of the following:

    - Printer Engine is set to PWG Raster or Apple Raster (URF).
    - The printer accepts that engine's format.
    - The requested duplex binding is actually advertised by the
      printer.

When enabled, MintPRINT sends one multi-page document per Print-Job
(PWG Raster or Apple Raster, matching whichever engine is selected),
which also works with printers that report
"multiple-document-jobs-supported=false". JPEG, PostScript and PDF
always print one-sided, regardless of what the printer itself
supports.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node ScalingQuality "Scaling and Quality"
@toc Main
                            Scaling and Quality
                            -------------------

Both dropdowns are filled directly from what Query finds the printer
advertises.

"auto" is the best general-purpose starting point for Scaling across
the printers tested so far - some alternatives (such as "auto-fit")
have been seen to get a job rejected outright on one printer, or
mis-paginate a single page across several physical sheets on another,
while "auto" printed correctly in both cases. Treat any other Scaling
value as something to try only if "auto" does not look right for your
particular application and printer.

The two explicit full-page choices have different purposes:

    Fit     Keeps the whole page visible while enlarging it as much as
            possible. With the PostScript engine, MintPRINT also respects
            any printable-area margins reported by the printer, so borders
            and other edge content are not placed in non-printable hardware
            areas.

    Fill    Enlarges the page to cover the physical sheet. Some content may
            be cropped at the edges. Printer margins do not change this
            cover/crop behaviour.

For JPEG, PDF, PWG Raster and Apple Raster, scaling is still applied
by the printer through IPP. The PostScript note above is specific to
MintPRINT's own PostScript page placement.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node Units "Multiple printers (Units)"
@toc Main
                         Multiple printers (Units)
                         -------------------------

If you have more than one network printer, MintPrint Settings can keep
a separate saved profile per printer, numbered Unit0 through Unit7.
The Unit dropdown at the top of the window lists all eight slots,
labelled from whatever has actually been saved:

    "Unit1 - Brother HL-L2350DW"   make/model known and saved
    "Unit1"                        saved profile, no make/model yet
    "Unit1 (empty)"                nothing saved in this slot

Picking a different Unit reloads the entire window - IP/host, path,
engine, media, cached capabilities, everything - from that Unit's own
saved files.

IMPORTANT: only Unit0 is what the installed MintPRINT driver actually
reads when it prints. Unit1-7 are switchable saved profiles, not
simultaneously active printers. To make a different Unit the one that
actually prints, select it and click Activate: this copies that Unit's
saved settings (and cached capabilities) over Unit0's, then switches
the dropdown back to Unit0. Activating overwrites Unit0's previous
settings, so save them to a spare empty Unit first if you want to keep
them.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node DriverInstall "Installing the driver"
@toc Main
                            Installing the driver
                            ----------------------

MintPRINT ships as one drawer containing MintPrintSettings and BOTH
compiled driver builds, under Drivers/:

    Drivers/MintPRINT-V44/MintPRINT    AmigaOS 3.2, 3.5, 3.9 and later
    Drivers/MintPRINT-OS31/MintPRINT   AmigaOS 3.0 and 3.1 (classic
                                        pre-V44 printer.device)

This is a real ABI split in printer.device itself, not just a build
option - see @{" Why two driver builds? " LINK "TwoDrivers"} for what
actually differs between them.

There is no separate archive to choose between AmigaOS versions.
MintPrint Settings detects which build this machine needs (from
workbench.library's version - or exec.library's if workbench.library
cannot be opened at all) and tells you what it found and which driver
it picked, in both the install/update prompt and the About window.

If DEVS:Printers/MintPRINT is missing (or an older revision) when
Settings starts, it offers to install/update it from the matching
Drivers/ copy above. After a successful install, Settings offers to
open AmigaOS Printer Preferences for you. In Printer Preferences,
select MintPRINT as the Printer Type and save - this last step is
always manual; Settings cannot select a driver in Printer Preferences
on your behalf.

Reboot after installing or updating the driver. A copy of the driver
already resident in memory will not pick up a replacement file until
then.

Prefer installing without running MintPrintSettings first? The
"Install" script at the top of the archive (a standard AmigaDOS
Installer script - double-click it, or run "Installer Install" from a
Shell) performs the same AmigaOS-version detection and lets you
confirm or override the driver choice before anything is copied. It
installs the driver and the MintPrintSettings drawer, and you still
need to select MintPRINT in Printer Preferences afterwards, same as
above.

On AmigaOS 3.0/3.1, both the classic driver and MintPrint Settings
itself require a bsdsocket.library V4-compatible TCP/IP stack
(Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami or similar) - Settings checks for a usable
socket before opening its window, and refuses to start without one.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node TwoDrivers "Why two driver builds?"
@toc Main
                          Why two driver builds?
                          -----------------------

This is a genuine change in the Amiga printer.device driver ABI, made
when AmigaOS introduced printer.device V44 (AmigaOS 3.2) - not
something MintPRINT invented, and not a mistake in this help file.
The split is documented in Commodore/Amiga's own printer.device
material (see the Amiga Developer CD 2.1 printer.device
documentation), and MintPRINT's OS3.1 driver build exists specifically
to bridge it.

What actually changed at V44:

    - Before V44 (AmigaOS 3.0/3.1's "classic" printer.device), a
      driver only sees the original PrinterExtendedData layout, and
      printer.device's own graphics pipeline only ever supplies
      4-bit-per-gun (0-15) Yellow/Magenta/Cyan/Black intensity values
      per pixel - the same depth the classic printer.device
      documentation's built-in 4x4 ordered-dither matrix was designed
      around.
    - V44 (AmigaOS 3.2 and later) added an extended tag-list
      interface a driver can opt into, including PRTA_8BitGuns
      (request full 0-255 intensity per gun instead of 0-15) and
      PRTA_NoIO (tell printer.device this driver never needs its own
      parallel/serial transport, since MintPRINT only ever talks IPP
      over the network).

MintPRINT's normal driver (Drivers/MintPRINT-V44/) uses that V44
extended interface and gets ready-to-use 8-bit colour straight from
printer.device. The classic build (Drivers/MintPRINT-OS31/) targets
the older ABI instead, so it only ever receives 4-bit values - and a
small compatibility shim (driver/classic_render_shim.c in the source
tree) expands each one to 8 bits (0x0->0x00, 0x1->0x11, ... 0xF->0xFF)
before handing that row to the exact same renderer, encoders and IPP
code the V44 build uses. Nothing past that point differs between the
two builds.

That is also why one binary can't just run everywhere: a driver built
for V44's extended interface has nothing to talk to on 3.0/3.1
(printer.device there has never heard of PRTA_8BitGuns), and a driver
built for the classic interface would leave a V44+ system's own 8-bit
colour path unused. MintPrint Settings' automatic detection (see
@{" Installing the driver " LINK "DriverInstall"}) exists so you never
have to work out which build you need by hand.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node TestPrint "Test Print"
@toc Main
                                Test Print
                                ----------

Test Print sends a small built-in test page straight through
printer.device to MintPRINT, using whatever settings are currently
shown in the window (saved or not). It is the simplest way to confirm
a printer actually produces physical output before trying a real
application - MultiView and Test Print both provide a much simpler
baseline than a strip-printing application like Wordworth.

An IPP job reporting success, or even a completed job state, is not
by itself proof of a physical page - some printer firmware accepts a
job and silently discards it. Treat an actual sheet of paper as the
real test.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node Troubleshooting "Troubleshooting"
@toc Main
                               Troubleshooting
                               ---------------

If a printer or a particular application does not print correctly,
the "printer compatibility page" (docs/PRINTER_COMPATIBILITY.md in
the MintPRINT source, and linked from the project's GitHub README) is
the best first stop - it lists confirmed working settings, and known
problems, for real hardware and applications already tested.

The Debug switch turns on driver logging to T:MintPRINT-driver.log,
plus a retained T:MintPRINT-job.* file (its extension matches whichever
document engine was used: .jpg, .ps, .pwg or .pdf). Turn Debug on only
while actively diagnosing a problem - leaving it on permanently is not
necessary for normal use.

Some general starting points, if a printer is new or is not printing
correctly:

    - Prefer port 631 and whatever IPP path the printer itself reports;
      "/ipp/print" is correct for every printer confirmed so far.
    - Start with Scaling set to "auto" if the printer advertises it.
    - If PostScript with Scaling set to "fit" still clips borders or edge
      content, include the full IPP probe output in the bug report so the
      printer's reported media margins can be checked.
    - Prefer an engine the printer actually advertises. Treat JPEG as
      suspicious if Query did not report any JPEG size/dimension
      capabilities for it, even though it remains selectable.
    - Test from MintPrint Settings' own Test Print, or MultiView,
      before trying a strip-printing application such as Wordworth or
      ArtEffect.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode

@node ReportingProblems "Reporting a problem"
@toc Main
                             Reporting a problem
                             --------------------

If Query reports that your printer supports none of the formats
MintPRINT can produce, or printing otherwise fails, please open an
issue on the project's GitHub page and attach:

    1. The output of windows_ipp_probe.py run against the printer from
       a Windows PC on the same network:

           python windows_ipp_probe.py http://<printer-ip>:631/ipp/print --all

       The --all flag requests every printer attribute and includes a
       full parsed attribute dump, which gives much better debugging
       detail than the default output.

    2. T:MintPRINT-driver.log from the failed attempt, with Debug
       switched on beforehand - see @{" Troubleshooting " LINK "Troubleshooting"}.

    3. The retained T:MintPRINT-job.* file from that same attempt, if
       Debug was on.

Use the report template on the printer compatibility page
(docs/PRINTER_COMPATIBILITY.md) so your result can be added with its
AmigaOS version, TCP/IP stack, engine and exact print options.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "Main"}
@endnode
